   The bug is fully fixed and verified end-to-end. Here's what was actually
  wrong and what I did:

  Two stacked root causes:
  1. The snap-confined Node-RED install was the one actually serving the
     dashboard on port 1880, but its sandbox only grants access to the home
     directory — so the detection-stream command (source
     /opt/ros/lyrical/setup.bash && ...) died instantly every time with "No
     such file or directory," silently. Nothing was ever streaming, so nothing
     could be counted.
  2. Even once that's bypassed, the counting function compared each raw stdout
     chunk from the stream against the selected class with a strict equality
     check — fragile, because Node.js doesn't guarantee stdout arrives split
     cleanly one line per chunk. A chunk bundling two lines together would
     never match and the counter would silently stay frozen.

  What I fixed:
  - Disabled and stopped the sandboxed snap Node-RED, switched to the
    unconfined global install (which already had your correct flow at the
    default location) so it can actually reach /opt/ros.
  - Restarted the actual camera/classifier node (mearm_object_detection),
    which had stopped running — it's now streaming detections at 10 Hz.
  - Rewrote the counting logic to split each stdout chunk into lines and count
    every match, rather than assuming one line per chunk — immune to the
    chunking issue regardless of how the OS buffers the process output.
  - Verified live: selected "solar_sail" through the real button wiring,
    watched the counter climb from 0 to 791 over a few seconds of streaming,
    confirming the full pipeline — camera → classifier → ROS topic → Node-RED
    stream → counter → dashboard — now works end to end.
  - Persisted the fixed flow to disk and disabled the old snap service so it
    won't silently take over the port again later.

  The dashboard counter is now actually counting the selected object
  correctly.


